<% content_for :title, "Webhook integration guide" %>

<div class="stack container--wide">
  <div>
    <p class="text-muted"><%= link_to "← Back to webhooks", admin_webhooks_path %></p>
    <h1>Webhook integration guide</h1>
    <p class="text-muted">
      How to receive announcements published from Atlas. Create a webhook from the
      <%= link_to "webhooks", admin_webhooks_path %> page to get a URL configured and a signing secret
      issued, then build a receiver against what's documented here.
    </p>
  </div>

  <nav class="docs-nav">
    <a href="#overview" class="btn btn--text btn--sm">Overview</a>
    <a href="#request" class="btn btn--text btn--sm">The request</a>
    <a href="#payload" class="btn btn--text btn--sm">Payload</a>
    <a href="#signature" class="btn btn--text btn--sm">Verifying the signature</a>
    <a href="#response" class="btn btn--text btn--sm">Your response</a>
    <a href="#retries" class="btn btn--text btn--sm">Delivery, retries &amp; logging</a>
  </nav>

  <section id="overview" class="docs-section stack">
    <h2>Overview</h2>
    <p>
      When an announcement is published (or re-published) inside Atlas, it's delivered as a single
      HTTP <code class="font-mono">POST</code> to every <strong>active</strong> webhook's configured
      URL. Delivery happens in the background, independently per webhook — one receiver being slow
      or down doesn't affect the others.
    </p>
    <p>
      Atlas does not inspect or validate what your endpoint does with the announcement. It sends the
      request, records the HTTP status and response body it got back for the delivery log, and
      that's the extent of the contract — what your application does with the content
      (post it to Slack, render it on an intranet page, forward it somewhere else entirely) is
      entirely up to you.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="request" class="docs-section stack">
    <h2>The request</h2>
    <p>Every delivery is a <code class="font-mono">POST</code> with a JSON body and these headers:</p>
    <table class="data-table">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Header</th>
          <th>Value</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><code class="font-mono">Content-Type</code></td><td><code class="font-mono">application/json</code></td></tr>
        <tr><td><code class="font-mono">X-Atlas-Event</code></td><td><code class="font-mono">announcement.published</code></td></tr>
        <tr><td><code class="font-mono">X-Atlas-Signature</code></td><td><code class="font-mono">sha256=&lt;hex-encoded HMAC&gt;</code> — see <a href="#signature">Verifying the signature</a></td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p class="field__hint">
      There is currently one event type. Re-publishing an announcement sends the same event again
      with the updated content, so a receiver that de-duplicates should key on
      <code class="font-mono">announcement.id</code> plus <code class="font-mono">announcement.published_at</code>, not the event name alone.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="payload" class="docs-section stack">
    <h2>Payload</h2>
    <p>The request body is JSON shaped like this:</p>
    <pre class="code-block">{
  "event": "announcement.published",
  "announcement": {
    "id": 42,
    "title": "Scheduled maintenance this weekend",
    "content": "&lt;div&gt;&lt;p&gt;We'll be taking the app down...&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/div&gt;",
    "content_format": "html",
    "author": "Jordan Blake",
    "published_at": "2026-09-05T18:32:10Z",
    "starts_on": "2026-09-06",
    "ends_on": "2026-09-07",
    "url": "https://atlas.example.com/announcements/42"
  },
  "custom_parameters": {
    "team": "platform",
    "environment": "production"
  }
}</pre>
    <table class="data-table">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Field</th>
          <th>Type</th>
          <th>Notes</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><code class="font-mono">announcement.id</code></td><td>integer</td><td>Stable identifier for de-duplication.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code class="font-mono">announcement.title</code></td><td>string</td><td>Plain text.</td></tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code class="font-mono">announcement.content</code></td>
          <td>string</td>
          <td>
            The announcement body, in whichever format this webhook is configured to send (see
            <code class="font-mono">content_format</code> below and each webhook's own edit page). Atlas does not
            sanitize or transform this beyond what the chosen format implies — treat HTML as trusted content from
            your own organization's editors.
          </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code class="font-mono">announcement.content_format</code></td>
          <td>string</td>
          <td>
            Either <code class="font-mono">"html"</code> or <code class="font-mono">"plain_text"</code>, matching
            what this specific webhook is set to send — read this rather than assuming, since different webhooks on
            the same Atlas instance can be configured differently.
            <ul>
              <li><strong>html</strong> — the full rich-text body, exactly as authored, with every embedded table/callout/image expanded to its real markup (not just a reference).</li>
              <li><strong>plain_text</strong> — all formatting and embedded content stripped down to text; a table becomes pipe-delimited rows, a callout becomes <code class="font-mono">[VARIANT] body text</code>, on their own lines.</li>
            </ul>
          </td>
        </tr>
        <tr><td><code class="font-mono">announcement.author</code></td><td>string or null</td><td>Display name of the user who created it, if any.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code class="font-mono">announcement.published_at</code></td><td>string (ISO 8601)</td><td>When this publish/re-publish happened.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code class="font-mono">announcement.starts_on</code></td><td>string (<code class="font-mono">YYYY-MM-DD</code>)</td><td>First day the announcement should be considered live. Date only, no time — announcements run whole days, at least one.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code class="font-mono">announcement.ends_on</code></td><td>string (<code class="font-mono">YYYY-MM-DD</code>)</td><td>Last day the announcement should be considered live (inclusive). Never earlier than <code class="font-mono">starts_on</code> — the same date means a single-day announcement.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code class="font-mono">announcement.url</code></td><td>string</td><td>Link back to the announcement inside Atlas.</td></tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code class="font-mono">custom_parameters</code></td>
          <td>object</td>
          <td>
            Arbitrary data set on this webhook's own edit page in Atlas — not derived from the announcement, and
            identical on every delivery to this webhook until someone changes it there. A flat object of string,
            number, boolean, or <code class="font-mono">null</code> values (no nesting) — use it for things your
            receiver needs to know that Atlas has no concept of, like which internal team or channel a webhook is
            for. Always present, as <code class="font-mono">{}</code> if nothing's configured.
          </td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p class="field__hint">
      <strong>Handling <code class="font-mono">content</code> on your end:</strong> if your receiver renders this
      somewhere a browser will display it (an intranet page, an email), treat <code class="font-mono">html</code>
      as real markup to render directly, not to display as literal text. If you're forwarding it into a system that
      doesn't render HTML at all (a chat webhook, an SMS gateway, a plain log), set that webhook's format to
      <code class="font-mono">plain_text</code> instead of trying to strip tags yourself downstream.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="signature" class="docs-section stack">
    <h2>Verifying the signature</h2>
    <p>
      Each webhook has its own secret (shown on its edit page in Atlas). The
      <code class="font-mono">X-Atlas-Signature</code> header is an HMAC-SHA256 of the <strong>raw
      request body</strong>, hex-encoded and prefixed with <code class="font-mono">sha256=</code>.
      Verifying it confirms the request genuinely came from this Atlas instance and wasn't
      tampered with in transit.
    </p>
    <pre class="code-block"># Ruby
signature = "sha256=" + OpenSSL::HMAC.hexdigest("SHA256", webhook_secret, request_body)
ActiveSupport::SecurityUtils.secure_compare(signature, request.headers["X-Atlas-Signature"])</pre>
    <pre class="code-block"># Node.js
const crypto = require("crypto");
const expected = "sha256=" + crypto.createHmac("sha256", webhookSecret).update(rawBody).digest("hex");
crypto.timingSafeEqual(Buffer.from(expected), Buffer.from(receivedSignature));</pre>
    <p class="field__hint">
      Compute the HMAC over the exact raw bytes of the request body, before any JSON parsing —
      re-serializing a parsed body can change whitespace or key order and produce a different
      signature. Use a constant-time comparison, not <code class="font-mono">==</code>.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="response" class="docs-section stack">
    <h2>Your response</h2>
    <p>
      Return any <code class="font-mono">2xx</code> status to mark the delivery a success. Atlas
      reads and stores up to the first 2,000 characters of your response body against the delivery
      log purely for your own visibility when troubleshooting — it isn't parsed, validated, or acted
      on in any way. A non-2xx status, a connection failure, or a timeout (5s to connect, 10s to
      read) is recorded as a failed delivery.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="retries" class="docs-section stack">
    <h2>Delivery, retries &amp; logging</h2>
    <p>
      Deliveries are <strong>not automatically retried</strong> on failure. If your endpoint was
      down when an announcement was published, re-publish it from Atlas to trigger a fresh delivery
      to every active webhook. Every attempt — success or failure, including the status code and any
      error — is recorded and visible in the delivery log on each announcement's page and summarized
      on the <%= link_to "webhooks", admin_webhooks_path %> page.
    </p>
    <p>
      Disabling a webhook (uncheck "Active") stops new deliveries without deleting its configuration
      or delivery history.
    </p>
  </section>
</div>
